MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

LangChain v1 middleware exposes `wrap_tool_call`, which receives the
model's proposed tool call before it executes and may return its own
`ToolMessage` instead of invoking the handler. That is a decision point's
shape exactly: decide first, execute only if permitted, report what
happened.

    conn, config = open_engine("agent.db", "policies.yaml")
    agent = create_agent(model, tools,
                         middleware=[OneDoorMiddleware(conn, config)])

This does not replace the `governed()` tool wrapper -- they cover
different graphs and share one engine and one policy file. Middleware
attaches to `create_agent` and governs every tool the agent has,
including ones added later, so it cannot be forgotten on one of them. The
wrapper works in a hand-built StateGraph with a bare ToolNode, where
there is no harness for middleware to attach to.

Two questions I could not answer from the docs, now answered by tests
that run a real `create_agent` loop against a scripted model:

- `interrupt()` works from inside `wrap_tool_call`. The graph pauses, the
  approval id comes back in `__interrupt__`, `Command(resume="approved")`
  releases exactly that action and nothing else. No `Command` return
  needed. Resuming re-evaluates rather than trusting the approval: an
  approval is permission to ask a second time, not a permit.
- `ainvoke` genuinely routes through `awrap_tool_call` -- verified by
  instrumenting both hooks, not assumed. The engine call runs in a worker
  thread rather than on the loop.

`open_engine()` exists because of a third thing the docs do not say:
LangGraph runs the tools node on a worker thread, and a SQLite connection
opened with the default `check_same_thread=True` raises
`sqlite3.ProgrammingError` there. That failure does not appear in a unit
test calling the middleware directly -- only in a real agent run. A
helper that opens the connection correctly is worth more than a sentence
in the documentation nobody reads until after the traceback.

Seven tests, all through the agent loop rather than against the hook in
isolation, so the assertions are about what LangChain does with what the
middleware returns. 129 pass. mypy unchanged at 7 pre-existing errors.
New extra: `pip install "onedoor[langchain]"`.
